            "id": "NP-004",
            "communityId": "wilmington-de",
            "title": "Delaware raises income threshold for Emergency Assistance eligibility",
            "summary": "Effective July 1, Delaware DHSS raised the income threshold for Emergency Assistance \u2014 which covers rent, utilities, and emergency shelter \u2014 from 100% to 125% of the Federal Poverty Level.",
            "body": "The Delaware Department of Health and Social Services (DHSS) announced that the income eligibility threshold for the Emergency Assistance (EA) program will increase from 100% to 125% of the Federal Poverty Level (FPL), effective July 1, 2026.\n\nThis change expands eligibility for thousands of Delaware households currently just above the cutoff. For a family of four, 125% FPL equals approximately $39,000 per year in 2026.\n\nEmergency Assistance provides one-time funds for rent, utilities, and emergency shelter for households facing an unforeseen crisis. Eligible households must also receive, or be eligible for, Cash Assistance (TANF, GA, or SSI) or certain Medicaid programs.\n\nThe change affects applications processed on or after July 1. Households that were denied EA benefits in the past 90 days due to income may reapply after July 1 under the new threshold.\n\nIn Wilmington, Emergency Assistance is administered through the Porter State Service Center (509 W. 8th Street) and the Northeast State Service Center (1624 Jessup Street).",
            "contentSource": {
              "id": "de-dhss",
              "name": "Delaware Health and Social Services",
              "type": null,
              "url": "https://dhss.delaware.gov"
            },
            "publishDate": "2026-06-02",
            "type": "policy-update",
            "urgency": "standard",
            "tags": [
              "emergency-assistance",
              "rental-assistance",
              "utilities",
              "low-income",
              "TANF",
              "SSI",
              "poverty-threshold",
              "health",
              "medicaid",
              "rent",
              "shelter"
            ],
            "why_it_matters": "This is a meaningful expansion. Many working families in Wilmington earn just above the old 100% FPL cutoff and have historically been ineligible for Emergency Assistance even during a genuine crisis. The new threshold brings more households into eligibility starting July 1.",
            "category_tags": [
              "Housing",
              "Utilities",
              "Rental Assistance"
            ],
            "subcategory": null
          },
